Let me look at the `loadProperty` function for `PROP_USER_UNIT`:

```cpp
} else if (type == PROP_USER_UNIT) {
    float factor;
    qint32 digits;

    xcf_io >> size >> factor >> digits;

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        char *unit_strings;
        xcf_io >> unit_strings;
        delete[] unit_strings;
        if (xcf_io.device()->atEnd()) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    size = 0;
}
```

The `xcf_io >> unit_strings` reads a length-prefixed string. The length is a `quint32`. If the length is very large, this could cause issues. But `QDataStream::readBytes` (which is what `>> char*` uses) allocates memory based on the length. If the length is huge, it could cause OOM, but that's not a memory safety issue.

